description:
The last unbuilt Stage-2 verb. docs/design-backlog.md:21 records it as the only one still open, and docs/vision.md names it in three places as a shipped-surface commitment:
 - :215 — a long description opens $EDITOR on 'create --edit', a no-title 'create', or 'edit' (journal entries never do — composed once via note add, ADR-0027);
 - :296 — listed among the Stage-2 verbs;
 - :315 — explicitly called out as one of the grammar block's intentionally-named not-yet-built verbs.

So the contract is already pinned; what is missing is the implementation. Scope:
 - 'tl edit <id>' opens $EDITOR on the current description, writes back on a clean exit, and makes no op when the buffer is unchanged (no empty setFields, no spurious updatedAt bump);
 - the machine path is never an editor — '--json' must refuse or take the value non-interactively, per vision.md:216;
 - decide and pin the no-$EDITOR and non-TTY behaviour, and the dirty-exit / empty-buffer cases, each with an error whose message teaches;
 - 'create --edit' and the no-title 'create' share the same editor path, so factor it once rather than three times.

Check first whether the two 'create' arms already exist: if they do, this is wiring a third caller onto an existing seam, not new machinery. If they do not, that is the real scope and this task should be split.

Invariant: every new production tap publication commit is signed by an explicitly selected release identity; ambient git configuration cannot change that policy; signing failure never results in an unsigned publication.

Code scope (this task). release/Homebrew.lean homebrew-publish keeps the existing git commit/push/remote read-back path and:
- reads a tracked signer record (committer name, email, SSH public key) named by a required --signer option; workflow-homebrew passes release/tap-signer.json;
- takes the private key from TL_TAP_SIGNING_KEY (key text, so CI needs no shell step to write a file), writes it to a private temp directory and removes it on every path;
- preflights before writing anything: key present, unencrypted (ssh-keygen -y -P "" never prompts), and its public half equals the pinned key; git >= 2.34 (SSH signing); otherwise a refusal with the fix;
- commits with every signing knob explicit (-S, gpg.format=ssh, user.signingkey, gpg.ssh.program, identity via -c and GIT_AUTHOR_*/GIT_COMMITTER_* env, core.hooksPath neutralised), no unsigned fallback;
- before pushing, verifies every commit the push would publish (FETCH_HEAD..HEAD, or all of HEAD when the branch is new) with an allowed-signers file holding only the pinned key: %G? must be G and %GK the pinned fingerprint. A pending unsigned commit from an earlier run is refused, not pushed;
- a genuine no-op (remote identical) and a prerelease stay no-ops that need no key.
Also: WorkflowRelease/WorkflowContracts/release.yml pass the secret; Prerequisites gains a carried row for it; ADR-0006 states the policy, the enforcement point (the pre-push check; Homebrew verifies nothing; a GitHub signed-commit rule is not a signer allowlist), and distinguishes artifact verification from tap-commit provenance.
Tests: production-shaped rows drop the fixture's commit.gpgsign=false in the tap checkout and use hostile local config; signed success verified against the expected public key; conflicting ambient key; missing/encrypted/mismatched key with no remote update; pending unsigned commit on retry refused; git < 2.34 refusal row under the git-floor job.

Not in this task: generating the real signer, GitHub account/key registration, the secret, and tap ruleset configuration — see the linked provisioning task. The production-signing requirement is not complete until that lands.
